/* ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   REFERRALS — rules to MERGE into the clearsky-portal ruleset
   ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   ⚠ THIS REPLACES THE EARLIER STANDALONE DRAFT. That draft carried its own
   userOrg() helper, which would have COLLIDED with the one already in your
   merged file — a duplicate function definition is a compile error, and a
   ruleset that fails to compile leaves the previous version running and says
   nothing. The block below defines no helpers at all.

   It also could not have worked as written: your userOrg() folds fenecon.de
   and fenecon.us onto fenecon.com via orgAlias(), and the draft's did not.

   ── WHAT TO DO ──
   1. Paste the Firestore block below into your merged rules file, immediately
      after the /projects block and before /sites. Nothing else changes.
   2. Deploy:  firebase deploy --only firestore:rules
   3. Search the LIVE rules text in the console for `referrals` before calling
      it done — same check the file's own header asks for on
      capacityAllocations, and for the same reason.
   4. Deploy the Storage block at the foot:  firebase deploy --only storage
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */


/* ═══════════════ FIRESTORE — insert after /projects ═══════════════ */

    /* ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       REFERRAL INBOX  —  referrals                                   ← ADDED
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       Quote requests. Someone on the platform has a site and wants a price on
       storage for it; the request lands in the receiving tenant's dashboard
       with the site, the ask, and whatever documents came with it. Written and
       read by omega-referrals.js on index.html.

       INSERT IMMEDIATELY AFTER THE /projects BLOCK, before /sites. It
       introduces NO new helpers — written entirely in signedIn(), userOrg(),
       ownsDemoBucket() and isOmegaStaff(), which already exist above. A
       standalone draft carrying its own userOrg() would have collided with
       yours and changed org resolution for every collection in this database.

       ── THE ONLY CROSS-TENANT OBJECT ON THE PLATFORM ──
       Every other collection here is scoped to ONE org and can be checked
       against the caller's own. A referral deliberately spans two: it is
       addressed by toOrgId and sent by fromOrgId, and both sides must be able
       to see it or the loop doesn't close. That makes this rule the whole
       security story rather than a formality.

       Doc body written by the module:
         toOrgId        the tenant it is addressed to   — required
         fromOrgId      the sender's OWN org            — required, pinned
         fromEmail      the sender, pinned to their token
         fromName, fromUid
         siteName, address, ask
         powerKw, energyKwh, product, stage, neededBy
         gridScore, bankableScore   0-99 or null, place it on the matrix
         status         new | reviewing | quoting | quoted | won | lost | declined
         docs[]         { kind, name, url, source, size, path, addedBy… }
         activity[]     append-only trail
         createdAt, updatedAt

       ── fromOrgId IS THE SENDER'S ORG, NOT THE TENANT'S ──
       ⚠ THIS BIT US ONCE ALREADY. The module first stamped fromOrgId from
       config.js, which on FENECON's portal reads 'fenecon.com' for EVERY
       signed-in user including ClearSky staff previewing it. userOrg() for a
       ClearSky address is 'clearsky-usa.com', the create clause below refused
       the write, and the only symptom was a dialog saying the rule was
       missing — when the rule was fine and the client was lying about who it
       was. omega-referrals.js now derives it from the signed-in email domain.

       ⚠ AND IT MIRRORS orgAlias(). The module carries its own ORG_ALIAS map
       folding fenecon.de and fenecon.us onto fenecon.com, because a .de user
       stamping fromOrgId 'fenecon.de' fails this clause outright. Same
       mirroring the Site Finder already does, and the same way it breaks: add
       a tenant alias to BOTH orgAlias() above and ORG_ALIAS in the module, or
       that tenant can read referrals and cannot send one.

       ── WHY isOmegaStaff() AND NOT isConsoleViewer() ──
       isOmegaStaff() is here because ClearSky reps work every customer's queue
       and sending a site to a manufacturer on a client's behalf is the job.
       isConsoleViewer() is deliberately absent: it grants sunesol.com and
       ogisolar.com a cross-org read, and a referral carries a site address,
       what the sponsor is willing to pay for, and the sponsor's own contact —
       which is a live prospect, not a drawing. Same reasoning as /sites.
       ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
    match /referrals/{referralId} {

      /* Is org `o` one the caller may act as? Own org, or a demo bucket they
         own. Deliberately does NOT fold in isOmegaStaff() — the two are ORed
         separately below so it is visible at each clause whether staff are
         included, rather than hidden inside a helper. */
      function refMine(o) {
        return o != '' && (o == userOrg() || ownsDemoBucket(o));
      }

      /* The recipient and the sender. Nobody else, including other tenants in
         this same Firebase project.

         THIS IS ALSO WHAT MAKES THE MODULE'S QUERY LEGAL. It asks
         .where('toOrgId','==',ORG), and Firestore refuses a LIST query the
         moment one returned document fails — so the queried org and userOrg()
         must be the same string. They are, because the module queries the
         tenant orgId and userOrg() folds the aliases onto it. */
      allow read: if signedIn()
                  && (refMine(resource.data.get('toOrgId', ''))
                   || refMine(resource.data.get('fromOrgId', ''))
                   || isOmegaStaff());

      /* Anyone signed in may send one, but only truthfully.

         Without the fromEmail check a user could file referrals in a
         colleague's name; without the status pin they could file one already
         marked 'won' in someone else's pipeline; without the empty-docs check
         they could attach a URL to a document nobody uploaded.

         .lower() ON BOTH SIDES. A token arriving as Menachem@ConcordEnergyUSA
         .com would otherwise be refused with permission-denied while everyone
         else in the same tenant worked — the single-user failure this file
         has already had to fix twice. */
      allow create: if signedIn()
                    && request.resource.data.fromEmail is string
                    && request.resource.data.fromEmail.lower()
                         == request.auth.token.email.lower()
                    && (request.resource.data.fromOrgId == userOrg()
                        || ownsDemoBucket(request.resource.data.fromOrgId)
                        || isOmegaStaff())
                    && request.resource.data.toOrgId is string
                    && request.resource.data.toOrgId.size() > 0
                    && request.resource.data.status == 'new'
                    && request.resource.data.get('docs', []).size() == 0;

      /* 1 · THE RECIPIENT works the referral: status, documents, activity,
             scores. It may not rewrite who the request is from or who it is
             to — that would let a recipient quietly reassign a live enquiry to
             another vendor, or claim one addressed to somebody else. */
      allow update: if signedIn()
                    && (refMine(resource.data.get('toOrgId', '')) || isOmegaStaff())
                    && request.resource.data.toOrgId   == resource.data.toOrgId
                    && request.resource.data.fromOrgId == resource.data.fromOrgId
                    && request.resource.data.fromEmail == resource.data.fromEmail;

      /* 2 · THE SENDER may keep attaching. The site map routinely arrives a
             day after the referral does, and making them re-send the whole
             request to add one PDF is how requests get duplicated.

             hasOnly() pins everything else, which is what stops a sender
             marking their own request 'won' in the recipient's pipeline, or
             quietly revising the ask after a price was given against it. */
      allow update: if signedIn()
                    && refMine(resource.data.get('fromOrgId', ''))
                    && request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
                         .hasOnly(['docs', 'activity', 'updatedAt']);

      /* A referral is the record of who asked for what and when. Close it as
         'declined' or 'lost'; the module has no delete path and neither does
         this. If one genuinely has to go, remove it from the Firebase console
         with admin credentials and expect to account for it. */
      allow delete: if false;
    }


/* ═══════════════ STORAGE — storage.rules ═══════════════
   Separate file, separate deploy. Without it uploads return
   storage/unauthorized. Link attachments (Drive, SharePoint) still work, so
   the block degrades to links-only rather than breaking — but it degrades
   quietly, so check for `referrals` in the live storage rules too.

   The path segment is the RECIPIENT's org: referrals/{toOrgId}/{id}/{file}.
   Written by upload() in omega-referrals.js.

     match /referrals/{orgId}/{referralId}/{file} {

       // Any signed-in user may read. The download URL already carries an
       // unguessable token, and the Firestore rule above is what keeps that
       // URL out of the wrong hands — a user who cannot read the referral
       // never sees the link. Constraining orgId to the caller's own org here
       // looks tighter and is not: it breaks the SENDER's access to the file
       // they uploaded a moment ago, because the path is named after the
       // recipient.
       allow read: if request.auth != null;

       // 25MB matches referrals.maxUploadMb in config.js. Keep the two in
       // step: the client check is a courtesy, this one is the limit.
       allow write: if request.auth != null
                    && request.resource.size < 25 * 1024 * 1024;
     }
   ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ */
